2:**********
3:
4:GNU ed is a line-oriented text editor. It is used to create, display, modify
5:and otherwise manipulate text files, both interactively and via shell
6:scripts. A restricted version of ed, red, can only edit files in the current
7:directory and cannot execute shell commands. Ed is the 'standard' text
8:editor in the sense that it is the original editor for Unix, and thus widely
9:available. For most purposes, however, it is superseded by full-screen
10:editors such as GNU Emacs or GNU Moe.
11:
12:   GNU ed is based on the editor algorithm described in Brian W. Kernighan
13:and P. J. Plauger's book "Software Tools in Pascal", Addison-Wesley, 1981.
14:
15:   If invoked with a FILE argument, then a copy of FILE is read into the
16:editor's buffer. Changes are made to this copy and not directly to FILE
17:itself. Upon quitting 'ed', any changes not explicitly saved with a 'w'
18:command are lost. In interactive mode, a non-existing FILE is reported but
19:does not alter the exit status.
20:
21:   Editing is done in two distinct modes: "command" and "input". When first
22:invoked, 'ed' is in command mode. In this mode commands are read from the
23:standard input and executed to manipulate the contents of the editor
24:buffer. A typical command might look like:
25:
26:     ,s/OLD/NEW/g
27:
28:   which replaces all occurences of the string OLD with NEW.
29:
30:   When an input command, such as 'a' (append), 'i' (insert) or 'c'
31:(change), is given, 'ed' enters input mode. This is the primary means of
32:adding text to a file. In this mode, no commands are available; instead,
33:the standard input is written directly to the editor buffer. A "line"
34:consists of the text up to and including a <newline> character. Input mode
35:is terminated by entering a single period ('.') on a line.
36:
37:   All 'ed' commands operate on whole lines or ranges of lines; e.g., the
38:'d' command deletes lines; the 'm' command moves lines, and so on. It is
39:possible to modify only a portion of a line by means of replacement, as in
40:the example above. However even here, the 's' command is applied to whole
41:lines at a time.
42:
43:   In general, 'ed' commands consist of zero or more line addresses,
44:followed by a single character command and possibly additional parameters;
45:i.e., commands have the structure:
46:
47:     [ADDRESS[,ADDRESS]]COMMAND[PARAMETERS]
48:
49:   The ADDRESSes indicate the line or range of lines to be affected by the
50:command. If fewer addresses are given than the command accepts, then
51:default addresses are supplied.
52:
53:2 Introduction to line editing
54:******************************
55:
56:'ed' was created, along with the Unix operating system, by Ken Thompson and
57:Dennis Ritchie. It is the refinement of its more complex, programmable
58:predecessor, 'QED', to which Thompson and Ritchie had already added pattern
59:matching capabilities (*note Regular expressions::).
60:
61:   For the purposes of this tutorial, a working knowledge of the Unix shell
62:'sh' and the Unix file system is recommended, since 'ed' is designed to
63:interact closely with them. (*Note GNU bash manual: (bash)Top, for details
64:about bash).
65:
66:   The principal difference between line editors and display editors is
67:that display editors provide instant feedback to user commands, whereas
68:line editors require sometimes lengthy input before any effects are seen.
69:The advantage of instant feedback, of course, is that if a mistake is made,
70:it can be corrected immediately, before more damage is done. Editing in
71:'ed' requires more strategy and forethought; but if you are up to the task,
72:it can be quite efficient.
73:
74:   Much of the 'ed' command syntax is shared with other Unix utilities.
75:
76:   As with the shell, <RETURN> (the carriage-return key) enters a line of
77:input. So when we speak of "entering" a command or some text in 'ed',
78:<RETURN> is implied at the end of each line. Prior to typing <RETURN>,
79:corrections to the line may be made by typing either <BACKSPACE> to erase
80:characters backwards, or <CONTROL>-u (i.e., hold the CONTROL key and type
[?2004hroot@68ab857d1493:/app# grep -nE '^[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)* |^Appendix|^Invoking|^Regular|^Addresses|^Commands' /app/docs/ed.txt
